  "worked_solution": "Let $R$ be the event that the pear is ripe, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the pear comes from basket $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the pear comes from basket $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the pear comes from basket $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A\u2294B\u2294C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a ripe pear, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a basket is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=P(C)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability of picking a ripe pear from baskets $A$ and $B$ are $\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\frac{1}{5},$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|A)=\\frac{1}{6},\\,P(R|B)=\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\n- Since the probability of picking a ripe pear from one of the baskets is $\\frac{1}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(R|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =(\\frac{1}{6})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{1}{3})+P(R|C)(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{18}+\\frac{1}{15}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{18}-\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) & =\\frac{23}{180} \\\\ P(R|C) & =\\frac{23}{60}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{23}{60}$ of the pears from basket $C$ are ripe.",
